## Why This Is Asked
Tests designing a mutable priority structure with time-based expiry and top-k queries, including updates and duplicates, under realistic constraints.

## Key Concepts
- Lazy expiry in priority queues to avoid frequent rebalancing
- O(log n) updates via delete+insert using an id map
- Tie-breaking with a unique id sequence to handle duplicates
- Top-k retrieval by temporarily extracting and reinserting items

## Code Example
```javascript
class Node {
  constructor(id, score, expiry, seq) {
    this.id = id; this.score = score; this.expiry = expiry; this.seq = seq;
  }
}
class DynamicPriorityQueue {
  constructor() {
    this.treap = new Treap();
    this.idMap = new Map();
    this.seqCounter = 0;
  }

  insertItem(id, score, expiry) {
    this.removeItem(id);
    const node = new Node(id, score, expiry, ++this.seqCounter);
    this.treap.insert(node);
    this.idMap.set(id, node);
  }

  updateScore(id, newScore) {
    const node = this.idMap.get(id);
    if (node) {
      this.insertItem(id, newScore, node.expiry);
    }
  }

  removeItem(id) {
    const node = this.idMap.get(id);
    if (node) {
      this.treap.remove(node);
      this.idMap.delete(id);
    }
  }

  queryTopK(now, k) {
    const result = [];
    const iterator = this.treap.inOrder();
    for (const node of iterator) {
      if (result.length >= k) break;
      if (node.expiry > now) {
        result.push({id: node.id, score: node.score});
      }
    }
    return result;
  }
}
```

## Memory Trade-offs
- Additional O(n) space for id map and sequence counters
- Expired items remain in memory until explicitly removed or overwritten
- Lazy expiry reduces computational overhead at the cost of memory usage

## Why This Is Asked

Candidates must handle time-decayed scoring with dynamic insert/erase and frequent top-k queries over a moving window, a realistic pattern in recommender and CDN analytics at large scale.

## Key Concepts

- Time-decayed scoring and exponential decay
- Windowed top-k queries with lazy deletions
- Maintaining running sums in a sliding window
- Trade-offs: lazy vs eager pruning, memory vs accuracy

## Code Example

```javascript
class TimeDecayStore {
  constructor(lambda) { 
    this.lambda = lambda; 
    this.map = new Map(); 
    this.heap = new MaxHeap(); 
    this.currentT = 0; 
  }

  insert(id, score, t) {
    this.map.set(id, { score, t_last: t });
    this.heap.insert({ id, decayedScore: score, t });
  }

  update(id, score, t) {
    this.map.set(id, { score, t_last: t });
    this.heap.insert({ id, decayedScore: score, t });
  }

  erase(id) {
    this.map.delete(id);
  }

  topKInLastWindow(k, t, W) {
    this.currentT = t;
    this.pruneExpired(t - W);
    const results = [];
    const temp = [];

    while (results.length < k && !this.heap.isEmpty()) {
      const item = this.heap.extractMax();
      const current = this.map.get(item.id);

      if (current) {
        const decayed = current.score * Math.exp(-this.lambda * (t - current.t_last));
        if (current.t_last >= t - W) {
          results.push({ id: item.id, decayedScore: decayed });
          temp.push({ id: item.id, decayedScore: decayed, t: current.t_last });
        }
      }
    }

    temp.forEach(item => this.heap.insert(item));
    return results;
  }

  sumScoresInWindow(t, W) {
    let sum = 0;
    for (const [id, { score, t_last }] of this.map) {
      if (t_last >= t - W) {
        sum += score * Math.exp(-this.lambda * (t - t_last));
      }
    }
    return sum;
  }

  pruneExpired(windowStart) {
    // Lazy pruning during queries
  }
}
